      "textEn": "217\n\nThey are chariots, gods, elephants, horses, and swords. Gajaindradbhirabhraabhirnrubhirvidyadharadyubhir. 7>. Vyarochant mahatejaah pathi kanchanamalinah. Divyastragvastrasannahah kalatrai khechara ivah. 8 ”. Meaning of the word:\n\nTe—they; Ratha—including chariots (of soldiers); Dev—of the gods; Dhishnya—airplanes; Aabhaih—in accordance; Hayaih—horses; Cha—and; Lavl—like waves (of water); Plavaih—whose speed; Gajaih—with elephants; Nadadbhih—with majestic elephants; Vidyadhar—of the Vidyadhara gods (like); Dyubhih—with speed;\nAbhra—clouds; Aabhaih—like; Nruhi—including foot soldiers; Vidyadhar—of the Vidyadhara gods (like); Dyubhih—with speed; Pathi—on the path;\nKanchan—of gold; Malah—wearing garlands; Divya—divine; Srak—of flowers; Cloth—cloth; Sannahah—as well as armor;\nKalatraih—with their wives; Khe-charaah—of the gods wandering in the sky; Iva—equal..\nThe mighty Yadu army passed through the path with great pride.\nAlong with them were their soldiers, who\nwere riding on chariots racing from the planes of heaven, on horses that were trotting, and\non huge and roaring elephants like clouds. Along with them, there were many equally radiant and skilled foot soldiers of the divine Vidyadharas. Adorned with gold necklaces and flower garlands, and\nthe Yadugan, wearing armor, were shining in divine attire in such a way that when they were moving forward on the path with their\nwives, it seemed as if the gods were flying through the sky. Having bathed there, the great fortunate ones, having fasted, are well-pleased. Brahmanebhyo dadurdhairnurvasah sragukmamalinih. 9. Meaning of the word:\n\nTatra—there; Snatvam—after bathing; Maha-bhagaha—the supremely virtuous (Yadava community); Uposya—after fasting; Su-samahitaha—with great attention; Brahmaneebhyah—to the Brahmins; Dadu—given as alms; Dhenu—cows; Vasah—clothes; Strak—garlands; Rukma—of gold; Malinih—and adorned with garlands.\nIn the Samantapanchak, those Yadavs with a saintly nature bathed and then, with utmost care,\nobserved a fast. Thereafter, they gave the Brahmins cows adorned with clothes, garlands, and gold necklaces in charity. Rama, with his heart, according to the rules, again, Vrishnayah. Dadaha swannam dvijachayebhyah krishneno no bhaktirastviti."
