      "textEn": "218\n\nMeaning of the word\n\nRam—of Parashurama; Hrideshu—in the lakes; Vidhi-vat—according to the rules; Punah—again; Aplutya—after bathing;\nVrishnayah—the Vrishnis; Dadu—given; Su—beautiful; Annam—food; Dvija—to the Brahmins; Achhayebhyah—the best; Krishnaya—to Krishna; Nah—to us; Bhakti—devotion; Astu—be; Iti—Thus..\n\nThereafter, the Vrishni dynasty, according to the classical orders, once again bathed in the lakes of Parashurama\nand fed the best quality Brahmins good food. At that time, he prayed this\nprayer, \"May we attain devotion to Lord Krishna.\"\"\nMeaning: This second bathing signifies the end of their fasting for the next day. The self, also known as Vrishnayah, is the divine Krishna.\nBhuktvopavisvishu kamam snigdhachhayanghripanghrisu. 11. Meaning of the word:\n\nSvayam—of oneself; cha—and; tat—by them (Krishna); anumantaha—permitted; vrishnayah—by the Vrishnis; Krishna—\nLord Krishna; devataaha—the only god; bhuktva—having eaten; upavivishuha—sat down; kamam—desire; snigdha—cool;\nchhaya—shadow; anghripa—of the trees; anghrisu—on the feet, below. Then, with the permission of their only deity, Lord Krishna, the Vrishnis performed Kaleva.\nAnd when they had time, they sat under the trees that provided cool shade. There they saw the friends of the kings, the well-wishers. Matsyashishnarakashalyavidarbhakurusrunjayan. Kambojakayanmadrankuntinartkerlan. 12. Anyanshai atmapakshiyamparansho shatsho nrupa.\nNandadinsuhrido Gopangopishchotkanthitaishchiram. 13. Meaning of the word\n\nTatra—there; Agatan—having come; Te—they (the Yadavas); Dadrushah—seen; Suhrut—friends; Sambandhinah—and relatives;\nNripam—to the kings; Matsya-ushinara-Kaushalya-Vidarbha-Kuru-Srijjayan—Fishes, O Ushinara, Kaushalas, Vidarbhas, Of the Kurus and\nof the Srinjayas; of the Kambojas—of the Kambojas and Kekayas; of the Madras—of the Madras; of the Kuntis—of the Kuntis; of the Anartas—of the Anartas; of the Keralas—of the Keralas; of the others—of the others; and of the birds—of the birds; and of the birds—of the birds; and of the birds—of the birds; and of the birds—of the birds; and of the birds—of the birds; and of the birds—of the birds; and of the birds—of the birds; and of the birds—of the birds; and of the birds—of the birds; and of the birds—of the birds; and of the birds—of the birds; and of the birds—of the birds; and of the birds—of the birds; and of the birds—of the birds; and of the birds—of the birds; and of the birds—of the birds; and of the birds—of the birds; and of the birds—of the birds; and of the birds The Yadavas saw that there were many kings who had come there, their old friends and relatives—Matsya,\nUshinar, Kushal, Vidarbha, Kuru, Srunjay, Kamboj, Kaikaya, Madra, Kunti, and the kings of Anarta and Kerala\ncountries.\nThey also saw hundreds of other kings who were both pro and anti. In addition to this, O King Parikshit, he, his beloved friends, Nanda Maharaj and the cowherds and"
